	There is a universal constant $C\geq 1$ with the following property.  For
	every supplied combined-point witness $Q$ with error $\delta_Q$ and every
	pair of opposite placements, sample $(\ell_X,x)$ and $(\ell_Z,z)$
	independently from the line-point distribution.  After completing each
	coordinate marginal with the zero outcome, the $X$ marginal at $(x,z)$ is
	within squared operator-family distance
	\[
		8\delta_Q+C\bigl(\eps+\delta_{\mathrm{Line}}(\eps)\bigr)
	\]
	of the evaluation of the $X$-line measurement at the first sample, and the
	$Z$ marginal satisfies the same bound against the evaluation of the
	$Z$-line measurement at the second sample.  The witness $Q$, its error, and
	the two independently sampled line-point pairs are retained as inputs; this
	is an auxiliary comparison and does not construct the combined line POVM.
\end{lemma}

